  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 241 PM CDT SAT AUG 1 2026\n\nFirst order of business will be assessing the potential for severe\nthunderstorms late this afternoon into this evening. Area radars\nwere showing a band of showers developing along and immediately\nahead of a cold front advancing into the area from the west. This\nis a little earlier (by a couple/few hours) than hi-res/CAMs \nshowed. The moisture, instability, and lift is available to\ncontinue to sustain convective development through the rest of the\nafternoon. Effective bulk shear values, however, are marginal at\nbest in Alabama at this time due to a more southwest (versus\nsouth) low level wind.\n\nThus, the current thinking for central Alabama is that we will\nremain on the southern periphery of the severe potential. As\nmulticellular convective extent and magnitude increases over the\nnext few hours, there's bound to be some cold pool consolidation \nwhich should be the main driver in storm organization. Damaging \nstraight-line winds are the main threat, and that will be highly \nconditional on how much forcing the cold front and associated\nupper level trough can provide to sustain persistent updrafts. Any\nsevere threat that does materialize should end in our area before\nmidnight.\n\nBeyond tonight, the initial upper low gets sheared out into a\ntrough, before cutting off again into another upper low that\ndrifts across the southeast states into early next week. Best rain\nchances during this time frame will be in the eastern half of the\nCWA. From the middle of next week onward, heights again rise and\nwe transition back to a more typical diurnally driven summertime\nweather pattern.\n\n/61/",
